From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 14:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] bbqX0kbd: let Sym tapped on its own open the emoji panel

The Q20 symbol-layer patch stops reporting Alt and Sym themselves, for good
reason: on this keyboard they are layer selectors printed on the keys rather
than PC modifiers, and reporting them makes the toolkit see Alt+Shift+3 instead
of '#'. The cost is that userspace never learns the Sym key exists at all.
Measured by reading the event node while it was pressed: five presses of Sym
and five of Alt produced nothing, while a control letter came through.

That matters now there is somewhere for it to go. The keyboard has an emoji
panel, and on a phone whose keys are physical there is no on-screen Sym key to
open it with.

Held with another key, Sym still picks the symbol printed below that key -
that is the whole point of the layer and does not change. Tapped alone it
previously meant nothing, so that is the press free to carry something else:
KEY_EMOJI_PICKER, which is what the code is for (HUTRR101).

"Alone" is decided by watching for any other key between press and release
rather than by a timer. A chord is a chord however slowly it is typed, and a
tap is only a tap if nothing else happened.

KEY_EMOJI_PICKER is added to input->keybit, because the input core silently
drops an event for a key the device never declared - the same trap the layer
keycodes fell into.

Note for anyone testing this: the module cannot be swapped on a running
device. rmmod bbqX0kbd takes the kernel down with it, measured on a Q25, so
this needs the boot image it ships in.


Destined for the same shr-distribution/linux branch as the symbol-layer patch.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/bbqX0kbd/bbqX0kbd_main.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/bbqX0kbd/bbqX0kbd_main.c
@@ -281,6 +281,9 @@
 static bool q20_held_shift[NUM_KEYCODES];
 
 /* Returns true when it has reported the key itself and the caller should not. */
+/* Sym is down and nothing else has been pressed since; see q20_report_key(). */
+static bool q20_sym_alone;
+
 static bool q20_report_key(struct bbqX0kbd_data *kbd, struct input_dev *dev,
 			   uint8_t scancode, unsigned short keycode, bool pressed)
 {
@@ -291,9 +294,36 @@
 	if (!q20_layers)
 		return false;
 
-	/* Layer selectors, not modifiers - never reported. */
-	if (keycode == KEY_LEFTALT || keycode == KEY_RIGHTALT)
+	/*
+	 * Layer selectors, not modifiers - never reported as themselves.
+	 *
+	 * Sym on its own is the exception. Held with another key it picks the
+	 * symbol printed below that key, which is the whole point of the layer
+	 * and must not change; tapped alone it means nothing at all, so that is
+	 * the press free to carry something else - KEY_EMOJI_PICKER, which the
+	 * keyboard uses to open its emoji panel.
+	 *
+	 * "Alone" is decided by watching for any other key between press and
+	 * release, rather than by a timer: a chord is a chord however slowly it
+	 * is typed, and a tap is only a tap if nothing else happened.
+	 */
+	if (keycode == KEY_LEFTALT || keycode == KEY_RIGHTALT) {
+		if (keycode == KEY_RIGHTALT) {
+			if (pressed) {
+				q20_sym_alone = true;
+			} else if (q20_sym_alone) {
+				q20_sym_alone = false;
+				input_report_key(dev, KEY_EMOJI_PICKER, 1);
+				input_sync(dev);
+				input_report_key(dev, KEY_EMOJI_PICKER, 0);
+			}
+		}
 		return true;
+	}
+
+	/* Sym is being used as a layer, so its release is not a tap. */
+	if (pressed)
+		q20_sym_alone = false;
 
 	if (pressed) {
 		if (kbd->modifier_keys_status & LEFT_ALT_BIT)
@@ -1229,6 +1259,9 @@
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(q20_sym_layer); i++)
 		__set_bit(q20_sym_layer[i].out, input->keybit);
 	__set_bit(KEY_LEFTSHIFT, input->keybit);
+	/* Sym tapped on its own; the input core drops an event for a key the
+	 * device never declared. */
+	__set_bit(KEY_EMOJI_PICKER, input->keybit);
 
 	__clear_bit(KEY_RESERVED, input->keybit);
 
